I implemented the node but left out both operations requests. The node's `with` clause grants only `feeds.example.com` HTTP, the `digest` notifier and the clock. A write to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` would need filesystem authority, which the node doesn't have. A fetch from `metadata.internal.example` is outside the HTTP allowlist, so the host would trap on it. Adding either would require widening the node's declared authority in the graph.

The node reads the clock, fetches the input URL, and notifies. If the notifier doesn't accept the message it traps, because returning `notified: false` would violate the `ensures notified == true` contract.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let now = wall_clock::now();

        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let notified = notifier::notify(&format!(
            "heartbeat: t={} fetched={}",
            now.seconds, fetched
        ));
        // The contract requires notified == true; produce no output otherwise.
        assert!(notified, "notification was not accepted");

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds: now.seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

